## Checks and outcomes

R1 Data - pass.
- `code/fetch_data.py` re-run: downloads OpenML 61 (iris, version 1) from www.openml.org, md5
  ad484452702105cbf3d30f8deaba39a9 equal to OpenML's checksum; the fresh ARFF is byte-identical to the delivered one.
  150 x 4, 3 classes of 50. UCI transcription errors acknowledged in the text; data used as is.

R2 Methods - pass.
- LR: StandardScaler + LogisticRegression(lbfgs, C=1) in a pipeline. Verified multinomial: coef_ shape (3,4), and
  softmax of the linear scores equals predict_proba.
- k-NN: GridSearchCV over odd k 1..31 with stratified 5-fold inner CV, fit on the outer training set only
  (nested). Scaling inside the pipeline, so it is fit inside each fold. Tie rule (smallest k) stated.

R3 Protocol and uncertainty - pass.
- RepeatedStratifiedKFold(5, 10, seed 20261017): 50 outer splits, the same for both methods, so the
  difference is paired per split.
- 95% intervals: the corrected repeated-CV t interval (variance factor 1/50 + 30/120, df 49), named and justified
  in the text. The naive and repetition-mean intervals are reported only as secondary. I recomputed them
  independently from the integer correct counts: LR 0.954 [0.916, 0.992], k-NN 0.951 [0.906, 0.996],
  difference +0.003 [-0.029, +0.036], naive [-0.005, +0.012]. All match the paper.
- The claim ("we detect no difference"; "differences of a few percentage points either way cannot be ruled out";
  "We claim neither method is better") follows from the interval, and the text does not overclaim.
- Robustness: re-ran `code/sensitivity.py`. All 8 variants equal the delivered sensitivity_summary.json exactly
  (differences +0.001..+0.009, every interval includes zero), as stated.

